September 14th 2017 a day that will  haunt Muhammad Yunus forever he says he  watched as Myanmar's security forces  burned his village to the ground  managing to capture this moment on his  mobile phone looking back as he ran they  came in the early morning and surrounded  the whole village they opened fire  randomly so we ran away then they burnt  our homes in front of our eyes we wanted  to find some of the people you see here  focusing on this man with a water bucket  to find out what happened to him after  asking around we were told where he  lives in this camp in southeastern  Bangladesh so he went to find him  Muhammad is now 20 years old he tells us  they secretly went back to their village  of Haledon after the military left to  salvage what they could I thought my  home is gone but I'll try to save the  other two houses it seemed we could have  saved they weren't totally burned so we  went back but he says the security  forces returned the next day and on  September the 16th they fled to  Bangladesh I'm always thinking about my  home all that happens keep coming back  to me who was killed who was shot I keep  seeing it in front of my eyes I have  lived in sadness for two years now I  miss my country a lot of the videos were  retrieved from mobile phones that had  been broken or damaged while people fled  across the river I was able to fix some  of the mobile phones about a third that  came to me but most of them were damaged  by salt water all of the videos that I  have seen showed that it was the  military committing these crimes another  video we found appears to show terrified  Ranga running from their burning village  gunshots can be heard  we're Hendra that gathered evidence on  their phones through photographs and  video footage their courageous people  they took great personal risk to gather  the evidence and some of this evidence  is is revelatory and exposes massacres  and violence that happen  in Rakhine State and should be used in  future prosecutions we asked if anyone  had approached them to hand over the  videos is some form of evidence we were  told no this camp has hundreds of  thousands of witness accounts and a  potentially crucial photographic and  video evidence of the crackdown against  the rahaga the International Criminal  Court is looking into opening a formal  investigation it's been two years and no  one has been brought to justice  Stephanie Decker al-jazeera in the  rahega refugee camps of Cox's Bazar
 

